## Introduction

In this lab, I worked with traps in xv6 to understand how system calls and interrupts are handled. I implemented a backtrace feature for debugging and added an alarm feature to periodically execute a handler function in user space.

---

## Lab Tasks

### RISC-V Assembly
I analyzed the assembly code in `call.asm` to understand how function calls and arguments are handled in RISC-V.

---

### Backtrace
I implemented a `backtrace()` function to print the call stack, which is useful for debugging kernel code.

---

### Alarm
I added a feature to xv6 that periodically alerts a process as it uses CPU time. This involved implementing two new system calls: `sigalarm()` and `sigreturn()`.

---

## Building and Running xv6

1. **Prerequisites**:  
   - I installed the RISC-V toolchain from [riscv-gnu-toolchain](https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain).
   - I installed QEMU for RISC-V (`riscv64-softmmu`).

2. **Build xv6**:
   ```bash
   $ make qemu

I used bttest to test the backtrace feature.
I used alarmtest to test the alarm feature.
I ran usertests -q to ensure my changes didn't break other functionality.
